candidates that is greater than the number cast for any other candidate but not more than half the total votes cast\nL74: ## Examples of plurality in a Sentence\nL75: \nL76: Recent Examples on the Web\nL77: \nL78: Examples are automatically compiled from online sources to show current usage. 【14†Read More】 Opinions expressed in the examples do not represent those of Merriam-Webster or its editors. 【20†Send us feedback†docs.google.com】.\nL79: Unless the governing documents provide otherwise, directors are elected by a plurality of votes cast. — Gary M. Mars, Miami Herald, 19 Oct. 2026 In 2006, in a notorious case, Pershing Square Capital Management, the hedge fund founded and managed by the billionaire Bill Ackman, had begun taking on a plurality stake in Borders, which operated about a thousand book-and-music stores. — Nathan Heller, New Yorker, 19 Oct.\nL80: 2026 Morrisey won the plurality in the Republican primary and went on to trounce his Democratic opponent in the general election. — Henry Culvyhouse, ProPublica, 19 Oct. 2026 Tickets still make up the plurality of MLB revenue, according to a previous Sportico analysis, but two-thirds of total team revenue today comes from other sources. — Jacob Feldman, Sportico.com, 8 Oct. 2026 【21†See All Example Sentences for plurality】\nL81: ## Word History\nL82: \nL83: First Known Use\nL84: \nL85: 14th century, in the meaning defined at 【22†sense 1a】\nL86: \nL87: Time Traveler\nL88: \nL89: The first known use of plurality was in the 14th century\nL90: \nL91: 【23†See more words from the same century】\nL92: \nL93: ## Browse Nearby Words\nL94: \nL95: 【24†pluralistic idealism】\nL96: \nL97: plurality\nL98: \nL99: 【25†pluralization】\nL100: \nL101: 【26†See all Nearby Words】\nL102: ## Cite this Entry\nL103: \nL104: Style\nL105: \nL106: [Select]\nL107: \nL108: “Plurality.” Merriam-Webster.com Dictionary, Merriam-Webster, https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/plurality. Accessed 29 Oct. 2026.\nL109: \nL110: 【27†Image†prod.cdn-m