e primary, one market gave Pratt a 78% chance of advancing to the November runoff. — Melissa Lee,paige Tortorelli, CNBC, 30 Oct. 2026\nL121: \nL122: Verb\nL123: Shadab completed his century by playing a late cut off Atkinson for his 12th boundary before hitting the fast bowler over long off for a six to reduce the target to three runs off the final three overs. — ABC News, 25 Oct. 2026 The Mercedes failed to turn at a curve and ran off the right side of the road, according to a crash log. — Kendrick Calfee, Kansas City Star, 24 Oct. 2026 【34†See All Example Sentences for runoff】\nL124: ## Word History\nL125: \nL126: First Known Use\nL127: \nL128: Noun\nL129: \nL130: 1867, in the meaning defined at 【35†sense 2】\nL131: \nL132: Verb\nL133: \nL134: 1680, in the meaning defined at 【36†transitive sense 1a】\nL135: \nL136: Time Traveler\nL137: \nL138: The first known use of runoff was in 1680\nL139: \nL140: 【37†See more words from the same year】\nL141: \nL142: ## Browse Nearby Words\nL143: \nL144: 【38†Runnymede】\nL145: \nL146: runoff\nL147: \nL148: run off\nL149: \nL150: 【39†See all Nearby Words】\nL151: ## Cite this Entry\nL152: \nL153: Style\nL154: \nL155: [Select]\nL156: \nL157: “Runoff.” Merriam-Webster.com Dictionary, Merriam-Webster, https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/runoff. Accessed 1 Nov. 2026.\nL158: \nL159: 【40†Image†prod.cdn-mw.com】Copy Citation\nL160: ## Kids Definition\nL161: \nL162: runoff\nL163: \nL164: 1 of 2 noun\nL165: \nL166: run·​off 【16†ˈrən-ˌȯf 】\nL167: \nL168: 1\nL169: \nL170: : a final contest or election to decide an earlier one that has not given the victory to one competitor\nL171: \nL172: 2\nL173: \nL174: : water from rain or snow that flows over the surface of the ground and finally into streams\nL175: \nL176: run off\nL177: \nL178: 2 of 2 verb\nL179: \nL180: ˈrən-ˈȯf,\nL181: \nL182: ˌrən-\nL183: \nL184: 1\nL185: \nL186: : to produce rapidly\nL187: \nL188: 2\nL189: \nL190: : to decide by a runoff\nL191: \nL192: 3\nL193: \nL194: : to driv