ional representation system. — Mauricio Savarese, Los Angeles Times, 30 Sep. 2026 Germany's electoral model is a combination of winner-take-all and proportional representation, meaning that parties need partners to form the parliamentary majority needed to govern. — Esme Nicholson, NPR, 21 Sep.\nL42: 2026 【17†See All Example Sentences for proportional representation】\nL43: ## Word History\nL44: \nL45: First Known Use\nL46: \nL47: 1870, in the meaning defined 【18†above】\nL48: \nL49: Time Traveler\nL50: \nL51: The first known use of proportional representation was in 1870\nL52: \nL53: 【19†See more words from the same year】\nL54: \nL55: ## Browse Nearby Words\nL56: \nL57: 【20†proportional rate】\nL58: \nL59: proportional representation\nL60: \nL61: 【21†proportional tax】\nL62: \nL63: 【22†See all Nearby Words】\nL64: ## Cite this Entry\nL65: \nL66: Style\nL67: \nL68: [Select]\nL69: \nL70: “Proportional representation.” Merriam-Webster.com Dictionary, Merriam-Webster, https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/proportional%20representation. Accessed 29 Oct. 2026.\nL71: \nL72: 【23†Image†prod.cdn-mw.com】Copy Citation\nL73: ## More from Merriam-Webster on proportional representation\nL74: \nL75: Britannica.com: 【24†Encyclopedia article about proportional representation†www.britannica.com】\nL76: \nL77: Love words? Need even more definitions?\nL78: \nL79: Subscribe to America's largest dictionary and get thousands more definitions and advanced search—ad free!\nL80: \nL81: Last Updated: 18 Oct 2026 - Updated example sentences\nL82: \nL83: ## More from Merriam-Webster\nL84: \nL85: #### 【3†Word of the Day】\nL86: \nL87: #### 【3†shindig】\nL88: \nL89: 【3†See Definitions and Examples】 »\nL90: ## Popular in Grammar & Usage\nL91: \nL92: 【4†See More】\nL93: \nL94: 【25†How to Use 'Who' vs. 'Whom'】\nL95: \nL96: 【26†Merriam-Webster’s Great Big List of Words You Love to Hate】\nL97: \nL98: 【27†'Affect' vs. 'Effect'】\nL99: \nL100: 【28†'A' or 'an'? What about before 'h'? An Indefinite Article Guide】\nL101: \nL102: 【29†Why is '-ed' sometimes pronounced at the end of a word?】\nL103: ## Popular in Wordplay\nL104: \nL105: 【8†See More】\nL106: \nL107: 【30†The Longest Long Words List】\nL108: \nL109: 【31†9 Words That Sound Like Insults But Aren't】\nL110: \nL111: 【32†12 Lovely and Unusual Words for the Natural World】\nL112: \nL113: 【33†Why do we 'bury the lede?'】\nL114: \nL115: 【34†'Za' and 9 Other Words to Help You Win at SCRABBLE】\nL116: \nL117: ## Popular\nL118: \nL119: 【8†See More】\nL120: \nL121: 【25†How to Use 'Who' vs. 'Whom'】\nL122: \nL123: 【30†The Longest Long Words List】\nL124: \nL125: 【31†9 Words That Sound Like Insults But Aren't】\nL126: ## Games & Quizzes\nL127: \nL128: 【2†See All】\nL129: \nL130: 【35†Quordle Can you solve 4 words at once? Play】\nL131: \nL132: 【36†Blossom Pick the best words! Play】\nL133: \nL134: 【37†The Missing Letter A daily crossword with a twist Play】\nL135: \nL136: 【38†A Monstrous Vocab Quiz Words That Go Bump in the Night Take the quiz】\nL137: \nL138: 【2†See All】\nL139: \nL140: 【39†Merriam Webster】\nL141: \nL142:   * 【40†Help】\nL143:   * 【41†About Us】\nL144:   * 【42†Apps】\nL145:   * 【43†Advertising Info†advertise.britannica.com】\nL146:   * 【44†Contact Us】\nL147:   * 【45†Privacy Policy】\nL148:   * 【46†Terms of Use】\nL149: \nL150:   *   *   *   * © 2026 Merriam-Webster, Incorporated\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRanked Choice Voting | NYC Votes (https://www.nycvotes.org/how-to-vote/ranked-choice-voting/)\nciteturn13view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.nycvotes.org/how-to-vote/ranked-choice-voting/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 144\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】 [Input]\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Skip to main content】\nL3: \nL4: 【2†English】\nL5:   * 【3†English】\nL6:   * 【4†عربى†arabic.nycvotes.org】\nL7:   * 【5†বাংলা†bengali.nycvotes.org】\nL8:   * 【6†简化字†simplifiedchinese.nycvotes.org】\nL9:   * 【7†正體字†traditionalchinese.nycvotes.org】\nL10:   * 【8†Français†french.nycvotes.org】\nL11:   * 【9†Kreyòl†haitiancreole.nycvotes.org】\nL12:   * 【10†हिंदी†hindi.nycvotes.org】\nL13:   * 【11†한국어†korean.nycvotes.org】\nL14:   * 【12†Polski†polish.nycvotes.org】\nL15:   * 【13†ਪੰਜਾਬੀ†punjabi.nycvotes.org】\nL16:   * 【14†Русский†russian.nycvotes.org】\nL17:   * 【15†Español†spanish.nycvotes.org】\nL18:   * 【16†اردو†urdu.nycvotes.org】\nL19: ## NYC uses ranked choice voting for local offices\nL20: \nL21: In primary and special elections for city offices, you can now rank up to five candidates in order of preference instead of choosing just one.\nL22: \nL23: 【17††www.youtube.com】\nL24: \nL25: View Transcript Hide Transcript\nL26: \nL27: There’s a new way for New Yorkers to have their say in city elections. A way that gives voters more choices and can lead to more diverse winners.\nL28: It’s called Ranked Choice Voting. 74% of New York voters chose to use it in primary and special elections for city offices like Mayor and City Council. You won’t see Ranked Choice Voting in general elections or elections for state or national offices. But in Ranked Choice Voting elections, you can now rank up to 5 of your favorite candidates for each office.\nL29: \nL30: Here’s how Ranked Choice Voting works:\nL31:   * On your ballot, you’ll see candidates listed in rows and numbered rankings in columns\nL32:   * Pick your 1st choice and completely fill in the oval next to their name under the first column\nL33:   * Like always, you can just vote for your one favorite candidate and submit your ballot\nL34:   * But, you might like several people\nL35:   * If you have a 2nd choice, fill in the oval next to their name under the second column\nL36:   * Do the same thing for your third, fourth, and fifth choices if you have them\nL37: \nL38: A few don’ts:\nL39:   * Don’t rank the same candidate more than once. It won’t help them and it takes away your chance to rank the others who are running.\nL40:   * Don’t give the same rank to multiple candidates. It could disqualify your ballot.\nL41:   * Don’t worry! This is a new process and you can always ask a poll worker for help, or for a new ballot if you make a mistake.\nL42: \nL43: So how do ballots get counted with Ranked Choice Voting?\nL44: If one candidate gets more than 50% of everyone’s first-choice votes, they win the election right away. That’s it! If no candidate gets more than 50%, ballots will be counted in rounds. Round by round, the candidate with the fewest votes is eliminated. So, if your top-rated candidate is eliminated, your vote goes to your next highest choice. This keeps going until only two candidates remain. The person with the most votes wins!\nL45: Ranked Choice Voting is already popular in many cities around the country because voters find that it helps more voices be heard. Now it’s our turn.\nL46: \nL47: Get answers to your questions and learn more at nyccfb.info/rcv.\nL48: ### Why do we use ranked choice voting?\nL49: \nL50: New Yorkers elected to use ranked choice voting in a 2019 ballot measure. It passed with 73.6% support.\nL51: \nL52: ### Which elections use ranked choice voting?\nL53: \nL54: NYC will only use ranked choice voting in primary and special elections for city offices including:\nL55: \nL56:   * Mayor\nL57:   * Public Advocate\nL58:   * Comptroller\nL59:   * Borough President\nL60:   * City Council\nL61: ### What are the benefits of ranked choice voting?\nL62: \nL63: There are several ways that ranked choice voting could benefit New York City voters:\nL64: \nL65:   * It gives you more say in who gets elected. Even if your top choice candidate does not win, you can still help choose who does.\nL66: \nL67:   * It gives you more choices. You can rank up to five candidates, allowing you to support your favorite candidate without worrying about whether they’re likely to win.\nL68:   * More diverse candidates win elections. Cities that have implemented ranked choice voting have elected more women and more women of color, making their elected officials more representative of their communities.\nL69: ### How to fill out your ranked choice ballot\nL70: \nL71: 【18†Image: Illustration of a Ranked Choice ballot†nycvotes-frontdoor-e6btczg8gpffcqd9.a02.azurefd.net】\nL72: \nL73: \nL74: \nL75: With ranked choice voting, you can rank up to five candidates in order of preference instead of choosing just one. Here’s how to fill out your ballot:\nL76:   1. Pick your 1st choice candidate and completely fill in the oval next to their name under the 1st column.\nL77:   2. If you have a 2nd choice candidate, fill in the oval next to their name under the 2nd column.\nL78:   3. You can rank up to five candidates. You can still vote for only one candidate if you prefer. However, ranking other candidates does not harm your 1st choice.\nL79: \nL80: 【19†Try a practice ranked choice ballot!】\nL81: ### How votes are counted with ranked choice voting\nL82: \nL83: Learn how votes are counted in ranked choice elections. Then view mock election results!\nL84: \nL85: 【20†Image: A ranked choice ballot with the candidates represented by animals†nycvotes-frontdoor-e6btczg8gpffcqd9.a02.azurefd.net】\nL86: \nL87: 【21†Learn how ranked choice ballots are counted!】\nL88: \nL89: ## Practice Ranking\nL90: \nL91: 【22†Image: NYC Votes animal candidates - close crop†nycvotes-frontdoor-e6btczg8gpffcqd9.a02.azurefd.net】\n"
